+there is a real nonzero $z\in U$ with each of these original means zero and
+$Q(z)<7c\norm z^2/16$. This particular canonical family therefore cannot
+restore target $c$ with any finite quadratic observation penalty.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Since $\gcd(\kappa,n)=2$, the orbit visits each even position twice.
+Thus $t_i=0$ occurs twice, $t_i=n-1$ never occurs, and all other $t_i$ are
+interior. There are four cells per channel at the two zero slices and six
+at each other slice. Counting all spatial rows in both channels gives
+$2\cdot3\{2\cdot4+(n-2)\cdot6\}=36n-24$.
+
+For interior $t_i$, the actual terminal second-coordinate endpoints are
+$t_i-1,t_i,n-1$; for $t_i=0$ they are $0,n-1$. The third-coordinate
+endpoints are $0,n-1$. Let $G_i$ be the six-entry tagged list obtained
+from $t_i,t_i-1\bmod n,n-1$, each paired with those two third-coordinate
+endpoints. It covers every actual corner, including the extra chart
+boundary. At $t_i=0$ some positions coincide but retain their tags.
+Six is a covering-list count, not an assertion of six cells at every slice.
+
+The identity $4\kappa=n+8$ gives a same-tag matching from $G_i$ to
+$G_{i+4}$: four entries translate by $(8,0)$ and two are stationary.
+Form the 24-entry window $F_i$ from $G_i,G_{i+1},G_{i+2},G_{i+3}$ and
