+\subsection{A separate counterexample for nonwrapping chart cells}
+The previous cyclic example does not imply failure of its chart refinement.
+We now construct a new cutoff that annihilates every refined mean separately.
+This gives a counterexample inside the original nonwrapping canonical class.
+
+\begin{proposition}\label{prop:chartrefinement}
+Let $n=2^m$, $m\geq5$, $\kappa=n/4+2$ and $t_i=\kappa i\bmod n$.
+At slice $i$ partition the second-coordinate chart into the nonempty members
+of $[0,t_i-1]$, $\{t_i\}$ and $[t_i+1,n-1]$, and the third coordinate
+into $\{0\}$ and $[1,n-1]$. Use these nonwrapping product cells in both
+original channels, with first coordinate $\{i\}$. Their exact full count is
+$36n-24$. If
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:chartthreshold}
+ L\geq2^{18}\cdot6\cdot148,
+\end{equation}
+there is a real nonzero $z\in U$ with each of these original means zero and
+$Q(z)<7c\norm z^2/16$. This particular canonical family therefore cannot
+restore target $c$ with any finite quadratic observation penalty.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Since $\gcd(\kappa,n)=2$, the orbit visits each even position twice.
+Thus $t_i=0$ occurs twice, $t_i=n-1$ never occurs, and all other $t_i$ are
+interior. There are four cells per channel at the two zero slices and six
+at each other slice. Counting all spatial rows in both channels gives
+$2\cdot3\{2\cdot4+(n-2)\cdot6\}=36n-24$.
+
+For interior $t_i$, the actual terminal second-coordinate endpoints are
+$t_i-1,t_i,n-1$; for $t_i=0$ they are $0,n-1$. The third-coordinate
+endpoints are $0,n-1$. Let $G_i$ be the six-entry tagged list obtained
+from $t_i,t_i-1\bmod n,n-1$, each paired with those two third-coordinate
+endpoints. It covers every actual corner, including the extra chart
+boundary. At $t_i=0$ some positions coincide but retain their tags.
+Six is a covering-list count, not an assertion of six cells at every slice.
+
+The identity $4\kappa=n+8$ gives a same-tag matching from $G_i$ to
+$G_{i+4}$: four entries translate by $(8,0)$ and two are stationary.
+Form the 24-entry window $F_i$ from $G_i,G_{i+1},G_{i+2},G_{i+3}$ and
+let $\eta(i,y)$ be the minimum of the associated radial functions $f_p$.
+The designated 18 shared entries remain stationary between consecutive
+windows; the six replaced entries have displacement at most8. Directly
+using~\eqref{eq:radial} and the minimum inequality gives
+\begin{align*}
+ \norm{1-\eta}_X^2&\leq4\cdot4\cdot6V/L^2,\\
+ \norm{\eta(i+1,\cdot+u)-\eta(i,\cdot)}_Y^2
+ &\leq\frac{64\cdot6}{L}
+       \{4|u|_1^2+16|u|_1+64\}.
+\end{align*}
+Without longitudinal shift the bound is $64\cdot4\cdot6|u|_1^2/L$.
+These explicit estimates remain valid with repeated centres. We have not
+applied the constant-cell-count theorem to this varying four/six-cell map.
+
+Set $\Psi=\eta\Psi_0$ and use the actual potential and field
+$z=(P\Psi,0)$ from the preceding proof. At every actual refined corner,
+$\eta$ and its backward transverse neighbours vanish. For each individual
+chart interval, its initial-endpoint predecessor is a terminal endpoint of
+the preceding interval, with $-1=n-1$ at the chart boundary. Every one of
+the four corners in the telescoped cell sum is therefore covered by $G_i$.
+Both needed first differences of $\Psi$ vanish there, so each separately
+normalized refined mean is zero. No zero sum on a union is used to infer
+zero sums on its pieces. The actual curl-adjoint formula proves real
+membership in $U$.
+
+For the convolution shifts used by $P$ and its derivatives, the translation
+factors are bounded by $A=\max\{64,36+48+64\}=148$. The same coefficient
+sums, now applied to the explicit six-centre estimates above, give
+\begin{align*}
+ \norm{P(\eta\Psi_0)-\eta z_0}^2
+    &\leq73728n\cdot4\cdot6/L,\\
+ \sum_{x\text{ odd},h}|\partial_h(P\Psi)(x)|^2
+    &\leq98304n\cdot6\cdot148/L,\\
+ \norm{P\Psi-z_0}^2
+    &\leq(64\cdot24/L^2+147456\cdot24/L)V.
+\end{align*}
+Threshold~\eqref{eq:chartthreshold} implies $L\geq2^{22}\cdot24$.
+The last coefficient is at most $2^{-38}+9/256<2$, so
+$\norm{P\Psi}>\sqrt{2V}$. The field itself is nonzero. Multiplying the
+strong estimate by the original $H=cn^2$ bounds strong energy by
+$3cV/8<3c\norm z^2/16$; weak energy is at most
+$12a\norm z^2=c\norm z^2/4$. This proves the strict $7c/16$ bound.
+All observation penalties vanish on the zero mean vector.
+\end{proof}
+
+This is a newly constructed invisible field for this particular
+nonwrapping refinement. It does not retroactively validate a split-cell
+inference for the old cyclic field. The threshold is enormous and
+unoptimized, and no general impossibility of canonical $O(n)$ partitions
+or global canonical logarithmic lower bound follows.
